Let $S$ be a semitopological semigroup (i.e., a semigroup with a Hausdorff topology under which multiplication is separately continuous). Denote by $C_b(S)$ the space of bounded continuous real-valued functions on $S$, by $AP(S)$ the subspace of almost periodic functions, and by $WAP(S)$ the subspace of weakly almost periodic functions. A mean on a subspace $X \subseteq C_b(S)$ containing the constants is a positive linear functional $m$ with $m(1)=1$; it is left invariant if $m(\ell_s f) = m(f)$ for all $s\in S$ and $f\in X$, where $(\ell_s f)(t)=f(st)$. The semigroup $S$ is left amenable if $C_b(S)$ has a left invariant mean.

Let $H$ be a Hilbert space and $C \subseteq H$ a nonempty closed convex subset. A nonexpansive representation of $S$ on $C$ is a family $\mathcal{T}=\{T_s : s\in S\}$ of mappings $T_s: C\to C$ with $\|T_s x - T_s y\| \le \|x-y\|$ for all $x,y\in C$, satisfying $T_{st}=T_s T_t$ for all $s,t\in S$. The action is weakly jointly continuous if $(s,x)\mapsto T_s x$ is continuous from $S\times (C,\mathrm{wk})$ into $(C,\mathrm{wk})$; weakly separately continuous if it is separately continuous in the same sense; weakly equicontinuous if $\{T_s : s\in S\}$ is equicontinuous on $(C,\mathrm{wk})$; and weakly quasi-equicontinuous if the closure of $\{T_s : s\in S\}$ in $C^C$ with the product weak topology consists of weakly continuous maps.

It is known that the representation $\mathcal{T}$ has a common fixed point in $C$ (i.e., some $x_0\in C$ with $T_s x_0 = x_0$ for all $s\in S$) provided there exists $c\in C$ with bounded orbit $\{T_s c : s\in S\}$ and one of the following holds:
(i) $C_b(S)$ has a left invariant mean and $s\mapsto T_s c$ is continuous from $S$ into $(C,\mathrm{wk})$;
(ii) $S$ is left amenable and the action is weakly jointly continuous;
(iii) $AP(S)$ has a left invariant mean and the action is weakly separately continuous and weakly equicontinuous;
(iv) $WAP(S)$ has a left invariant mean and the action is weakly separately continuous and weakly quasi-equicontinuous.

Question: In each of cases (i)–(iv), does the converse hold? That is, if every nonexpansive representation of $S$ on a nonempty closed convex subset $C$ of a Hilbert space possessing some element with bounded orbit and satisfying the stated continuity/equicontinuity hypothesis admits a common fixed point in $C$, does it follow that the corresponding invariance/amenability hypothesis on $S$ holds?

